<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coptos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qift, Koptos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coptos, modern Qift, was an ancient Egyptian city on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. It was the starting point of desert routes through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hammamat">Wadi Hammamat</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and to stone quarries and gold mines, and the cult centre of the fertility god Min. Royal decrees of the late Old Kingdom found there record privileges granted to its temple and to powerful local officials.</p></div>
